atial vision, leadership. Concerns: show-off (maybe cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). No info on stress, reliability, communication.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation, communication). So strengths: reliability, stress, technical. Concerns: memory, interpersonal.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated technology (technical), difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So strengths: decision, stress, technical. Concerns: communication, egocentric, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress/resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but regarded as know-it-all (crew cooperation), has a hot temper (stress/cooperation). So strengths: problem solving, concentration, responsibility. Concerns: hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have concerns. Which candidate has most strengths and least severe concerns? Candidate B has conscientiousness and stress handling, but nasty remarks is serious for crew cooperation. Candidate C has calm, quick decisions, but egocentric and communication issues. Candidate D has responsibility, concentration, problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all may affect crew cooperation. Candidate A has anticipation and leadership but show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nWhich is most balanced? Maybe Candidate B: reliability and stress are crucial, and technical competence is good. Interpersonal issue is serious but could be mitigated? However, nasty rem